1
00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:16,897
Destiny. Fate. Dreams.

2
00:00:16,980 --> 00:00:21,140
These unstoppable ideals are
held deep in the heart of man.

3
00:00:21,540 --> 00:00:24,937
As long as there are people
who seek freedom in this life,

4
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:27,667
these things shall not
vanish from the earth.

5
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:37,497
People tell me my head's in the clouds.

6
00:00:37,580 --> 00:00:40,897
I don't care what they say
because I'm dreaming of you.

7
00:00:40,980 --> 00:00:43,557
You are the treasure I found.

8
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:46,657
So bright, so bold, and so elusive.

9
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:48,737
It's hard to look away.

10
00:00:48,820 --> 00:00:51,877
Even though it's blinding me.

11
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:55,697
There are no words to
describe how I feel inside.

12
00:00:55,780 --> 00:00:58,020
I'm really, really stuck on you.

13
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:02,537
I have to follow my dreams,
however crazy they seem.

14
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:05,577
And I won't stop till I
get there, you will see.

15
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:08,877
You're all I need to have
the strength to believe.

16
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:12,220
We live in wonderland.

17
00:01:17,940 --> 00:01:21,117
I'm gonna follow my dreams,
however crazy they seem.

18
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:24,157
I'm gonna share them
with you, a love so true.

19
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:27,557
You're all I want,
together we can move on.

20
00:01:27,640 --> 00:01:29,917
We live how we want to.

21
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,037
There is no limit to us.

22
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:33,537
We have the power of love.

23
00:01:33,620 --> 00:01:36,637
We don't want to live ordinary life.

24
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:39,757
We have a chance to live in paradise.

25
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:42,480
We live in one another.

26
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:08,320
It's a...

27
00:02:09,260 --> 00:02:10,090
Flower.

28
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:14,900
No, it's a person.

29
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:16,240
Are you sure?

30
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:20,100
That old man just killed a
great king squid with one shot.

31
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:21,680
The question is, why?

32
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:24,904
Was he trying to save us,
or is he just fishing?

33
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:53,680
You're gonna say anything?

34
00:02:57,640 --> 00:02:58,877
Hey, you want to fight?

35
00:02:58,960 --> 00:02:59,790
We'll fight.

36
00:02:59,873 --> 00:03:02,020
We got cannons back here! You bastard!

37
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:15,700
You try it and someone's going to die!

38
00:03:19,620 --> 00:03:21,517
Yeah, and who's that?

39
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:22,757
Me, of course.

40
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:23,857
Excuse me.

41
00:03:23,940 --> 00:03:24,840
You?

42
00:03:25,060 --> 00:03:26,178
He's up there, bud.

43
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:28,065
That old bastard's mocking us.

44
00:03:29,560 --> 00:03:31,717
There's a couple things we need to know.

45
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:32,900
Where are we?

46
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:34,640
And who are you?

47
00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:45,260
You've got some nerve, stranger.

48
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:47,717
It's only polite to
introduce yourself before

49
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:50,447
asking a question of
someone you've just met.

50
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:53,380
All right, fair enough.

51
00:03:53,940 --> 00:03:54,780
I'll go first.

52
00:03:54,980 --> 00:03:55,980
If you must know,

53
00:03:56,180 --> 00:04:00,400
my name is Crocus, and I'm the
lighthouse keeper of the Twin Capes.

54
00:04:00,540 --> 00:04:03,180
I'm 71 and a half years old, Gemini.

55
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:04,580
I'm a sword!

56
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:07,142
You'd like to know where we are,

57
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:11,957
but your rude behavior leaves me
disinclined to accommodate your curiosity?

58
00:04:12,040 --> 00:04:15,320
except that you are trespassing
on my private resort.

59
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:20,441
Besides, I think it's pretty obvious,
seeing as how you were eaten.

60
00:04:20,840 --> 00:04:23,357
So you're saying that we
were swallowed by the whale.

61
00:04:23,440 --> 00:04:26,317
But since when does a whale's
stomach have clouds in it?

62
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:29,217
Wait a minute, if we've been eaten,
what's going to happen to us?

63
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:30,947
I don't want to be digested!

64
00:04:39,660 --> 00:04:42,000
Stop doing that! What's wrong?

65
00:04:42,140 --> 00:04:44,157
Haven't you ever heard of a running gag?

66
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:45,122
That was a gag!

67
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:46,960
The exit is over there.

68
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:48,162
We can get out!

69
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:52,884
Okay, even if I believed a whale's
belly could have an exit door?

70
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:54,852
Nothing's there but sky.

71
00:04:55,680 --> 00:04:57,268
Something's not right here.

72
00:04:58,140 --> 00:04:59,680
Wait, look at the sky.

73
00:05:00,040 --> 00:05:02,200
No Scales. Nothing's moving.

74
00:05:02,680 --> 00:05:03,800
They're painted on.

75
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
It's a giant mural inside
the whale's stomach. Huh?

76
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:09,217
Call it a doctor's playful mind.

77
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:11,677
You're not making any
sense, you crazy old fool.

78
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:13,277
Easy, don't let him get to you.

79
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:14,520
Yeah, just shut up.

80
00:05:14,620 --> 00:05:17,417
Nami, if that is an exit,
then we need to get to it.

81
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:18,330
Agreed.

82
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:24,560
Ah, Crate, what now?

83
00:05:25,940 --> 00:05:26,860
It's begun

84
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:30,290
Look!

85
00:05:30,373 --> 00:05:32,372
That's no island, it's some kind of ship!

86
00:05:32,620 --> 00:05:33,679
It's made of wire!

87
00:05:34,140 --> 00:05:35,057
Yeah, of course!

88
00:05:35,140 --> 00:05:37,277
Because we're floating
on a sea of gastric acid!

89
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:41,657
That means a wooden ship wouldn't fare
well if it stayed here too long Hey,

90
00:05:41,740 --> 00:05:43,505
what's with this freaky whale?

91
00:05:43,780 --> 00:05:45,200
Answer me, old man!

92
00:05:45,640 --> 00:05:47,040
He's very angry.

93
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:53,271
These aftershocks are a result of him
hitting his head against the red line.

94
00:06:02,460 --> 00:06:03,290
What?

95
00:06:03,373 --> 00:06:04,660
That explains it?

96
00:06:05,020 --> 00:06:06,937
Why he had such deep
scars on his forehead!

97
00:06:07,020 --> 00:06:09,297
Not to mention all this strange howling!

98
00:06:09,380 --> 00:06:11,180
Why does he do it?

99
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:13,460
It's because he's suffering.

100
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:23,157
It's all starting to make sense
so what's the geezer up to?

101
00:06:23,240 --> 00:06:25,857
More than likely he's trying to kill.

102
00:06:25,940 --> 00:06:27,117
The whale from the inside.

103
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:28,917
It's time.

104
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:30,897
What a despicable way to kill something.

105
00:06:30,980 --> 00:06:32,897
Okay we've Solved the
mystery of the whale.

106
00:06:32,980 --> 00:06:35,917
Now how about we find a way out
of its belly before it digests us?

107
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,837
We have no right to judge the old
man nor do we need to save the whale.

108
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:42,997
What we need to do is Focus on
how to get the hell out of here.

109
00:06:43,080 --> 00:06:50,080
We're

110
00:06:57,340 --> 00:06:58,170
never gonna

111
00:06:58,253 --> 00:06:59,083
get anywhere if

112
00:06:59,166 --> 00:07:00,077
the Waves keep searching like this?

113
00:07:00,160 --> 00:07:01,417
How can we escape?

114
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:02,577
If we can't even move.

115
00:07:02,660 --> 00:07:04,037
Our only Choice is to paddle.

116
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:05,117
Take the oars.

117
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:06,177
Whatever it takes.

118
00:07:06,260 --> 00:07:07,117
We have to fight.

119
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:08,057
Luffy, right away!

120
00:07:08,140 --> 00:07:09,316
I know he's outside!

121
00:07:09,440 --> 00:07:12,852
I caught a glimpse of him
flying out of the whale's mouth!

122
00:07:14,040 --> 00:07:16,700
That old guy just jumped in! Huh?

123
00:07:16,920 --> 00:07:18,080
Is he insane?

124
00:07:18,440 --> 00:07:20,087
The acid will eat him alive!

125
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:35,540
I'm so dizzy, I'm gonna hurl!

126
00:07:44,280 --> 00:07:45,600
Hey, that's weird.

127
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:48,134
The whale's got a river inside it, too.

128
00:07:57,860 --> 00:07:59,740
Things are looking up, my dear.

129
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:01,700
We've been successful so far.

130
00:08:01,960 --> 00:08:03,697
Listen carefully, Miss Wednesday.

131
00:08:03,780 --> 00:08:08,700
Behind these doors lie the beast's stomach,
and that troublesome geezer as well.

132
00:08:08,960 --> 00:08:12,019
He must be eliminated before
he can interfere again.

133
00:08:13,140 --> 00:08:16,500
Remember, it's all for the
whale. Indeed, Mr. Nine.

134
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:18,657
This whale's a precious creature,

135
00:08:18,740 --> 00:08:22,152
and one that holds the sweet
promise of life for our town.

136
00:08:24,220 --> 00:08:25,050
Guys!

137
00:08:25,133 --> 00:08:26,277
Crocus just resurfaced!

138
00:08:26,360 --> 00:08:27,897
Look, forget about the old guy.

139
00:08:27,980 --> 00:08:31,333
Our focus should be on finding
a way to open these doors.

140
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:33,540
All right, here's the plan, baby.

141
00:08:34,240 --> 00:08:37,220
Three, two, one, then we
kick through the doors!

142
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:38,840
Roger that, Mr. Nine.

143
00:08:39,180 --> 00:08:40,298
I'm ready any time.

144
00:08:43,700 --> 00:08:45,620
What is that voice, Mr. Nine?

145
00:08:45,800 --> 00:08:47,506
I don't know, Miss Wednesday.

146
00:08:58,360 --> 00:08:59,320
They're back!

147
00:09:02,140 --> 00:09:03,000
Not good!

148
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:05,475
We're going to fall into gastric acid!

149
00:09:07,860 --> 00:09:08,690
Luffy?

150
00:09:08,773 --> 00:09:10,060
Zoro, is everyone okay?

151
00:09:12,380 --> 00:09:13,317
So, um...

152
00:09:13,400 --> 00:09:15,180
I can use a hand!

153
00:09:19,220 --> 00:09:20,917
Looks like Luffy's not alone.

154
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,077
Who do you suppose those
two weirdos are with him?

155
00:09:23,160 --> 00:09:25,297
Where'd that old geezer disappear to?

156
00:09:25,380 --> 00:09:26,210
Forget him.

157
00:09:26,293 --> 00:09:27,474
We need to save Luffy.

158
00:09:42,740 --> 00:09:44,640
You've got to stop it, Laboon.

159
00:09:44,880 --> 00:09:47,517
You can't afford to beat
yourself up anymore.

160
00:09:47,600 --> 00:09:50,188
There's only a tiny
amount of sedative left,

161
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:53,633
and until I can make some
more, you'll have to make nuts.

162
00:09:55,600 --> 00:09:59,237
I've told you before, that wall
divides the world's oceans.

163
00:09:59,320 --> 00:10:01,940
It doesn't matter how
many times you hit it.

164
00:10:02,100 --> 00:10:05,276
It won't break, not even for
someone as mighty as you.

165
00:10:09,300 --> 00:10:12,657
I don't claim to speak whale, but
it seems to have calmed down a bit.

166
00:10:12,740 --> 00:10:13,622
Seems that way.

167
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:21,437
Now, we saved your lives for the time being,
but you better talk and make it quick.

168
00:10:21,520 --> 00:10:22,350
Mr.

169
00:10:22,433 --> 00:10:24,120
Nine, these heathens are pirates.

170
00:10:24,440 --> 00:10:26,657
Yes, that's painfully
obvious, Miss Wednesday.

171
00:10:26,740 --> 00:10:30,797
But if we speak to their humanity and
compassion, they should understand our plight.

172
00:10:30,880 --> 00:10:31,710
Maybe.

173
00:10:31,960 --> 00:10:34,180
You parasites are still here.

174
00:10:34,580 --> 00:10:37,460
I grow weary of this,
and for the last time,

175
00:10:37,720 --> 00:10:43,020
as long as I draw breath, you will not
lay a single harmful finger on Laboon.

176
00:10:45,180 --> 00:10:46,320
He's back again.

177
00:10:46,640 --> 00:10:47,699
Who's the old guy?

178
00:10:49,800 --> 00:10:52,565
You can't bully us into
abandoning our mission.

179
00:10:52,720 --> 00:10:56,137
We were sent here to hunt this whale, and
that's exactly what we're going to do.

180
00:10:56,220 --> 00:10:58,632
And this time we won't let you interfere.

181
00:10:58,860 --> 00:11:01,566
We're about to give this
whale a new blowhole!

182
00:11:01,720 --> 00:11:02,600
Fire, baby!

183
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:04,030
Let's...

184
00:11:15,240 --> 00:11:18,577
He jumped straight into the
line of fire! Did he just...

185
00:11:18,660 --> 00:11:20,307
Was he protecting the whale?

186
00:11:23,560 --> 00:11:25,840
Your defiance is pointless. Go ahead!

187
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:26,960
Waste your time!

188
00:11:27,480 --> 00:11:31,920
But make no mistake, that hulking
beast will provide food for our town!

189
00:11:37,340 --> 00:11:38,640
What's going on?

190
00:11:45,040 --> 00:11:46,400
What was that for?

191
00:11:46,580 --> 00:11:47,577
I just...

192
00:11:47,660 --> 00:11:48,880
wanted to hit him.

193
00:11:53,760 --> 00:11:57,717
I thank you for saving my life,
though I must ask, why did you do it?

194
00:11:57,800 --> 00:11:59,447
I wasn't trying to save you.

195
00:11:59,560 --> 00:12:02,795
There was just something
about those two I didn't like.

196
00:12:02,920 --> 00:12:05,337
Okay, I think it's time
we got some answers.

197
00:12:05,420 --> 00:12:06,477
Who are these guys?

198
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:08,737
And how did you end up inside this whale?

199
00:12:08,820 --> 00:12:12,317
These two are thugs from a nearby
town looking for whale meat.

200
00:12:12,400 --> 00:12:13,457
If they caught him,

201
00:12:13,540 --> 00:12:16,917
Laboon could feed the townspeople
for at least two or three years easy.

202
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:20,706
All they're concerned with is
filling their fat, greedy bellies

203
00:12:21,400 --> 00:12:22,230
Laboon?

204
00:12:22,313 --> 00:12:23,637
That's the whale's name.

205
00:12:23,720 --> 00:12:26,257
He's a unique and magnificent creature.

206
00:12:26,340 --> 00:12:29,837
An island whale, one of the
largest species in the world.

207
00:12:29,920 --> 00:12:31,897
That can only be found in the West Blue.

208
00:12:31,980 --> 00:12:34,857
I won't allow them to
slaughter him for food.

209
00:12:34,940 --> 00:12:38,717
It's a shame that he's stuck
here so far away from his domain.

210
00:12:38,800 --> 00:12:42,277
You see, there's a reason he keeps
hitting himself on the red line.

211
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:44,242
And howling at Reverse Mountain.

212
00:12:44,540 --> 00:12:45,417
There is?

213
00:12:45,500 --> 00:12:47,020
Yes,

214
00:12:47,380 --> 00:12:53,017
to put it one way Inside Laboon's
body beats the heart of a human He has

215
00:12:53,100 --> 00:12:58,680
most impatiently awaited And is eternally
devoted to a certain band of pirates

216
00:13:01,120 --> 00:13:05,657
Going on 50 long years now Listen closely

217
00:13:05,740 --> 00:13:09,920
And I will reveal to you
Laboon's tragic story

218
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:27,897
One fine sunny day While I was fulfilling
my duties as the lighthouse keeper Some

219
00:13:27,980 --> 00:13:33,560
good-natured pirates came down Reverse
Mountain They were followed by a baby whale,

220
00:13:33,760 --> 00:13:39,260
Spry and Chipper Whom I would soon
come to know as the one and only Laboon

221
00:13:40,800 --> 00:13:42,480
He is an island whale,

222
00:13:42,660 --> 00:13:47,640
one of the largest in the world They are a
rare species found only in the West Blue.

223
00:13:48,460 --> 00:13:50,997
Laboon had developed a
bond with the pirates,

224
00:13:51,080 --> 00:13:53,197
traveling with them for
much of their journey.

225
00:13:53,280 --> 00:13:55,517
But knowing the dangers of the Grand Line,

226
00:13:55,600 --> 00:13:59,012
the pirates chose to leave
Laboon behind in the West Blue.

227
00:13:59,220 --> 00:14:02,400
But the stubborn little
whale followed them anyway.

228
00:14:02,780 --> 00:14:04,057
As a general rule,

229
00:14:04,140 --> 00:14:08,258
island whales are social animals and
travel together in family groups.

230
00:14:08,440 --> 00:14:13,264
I suspect that Laboon had somehow come to
feel that those pirates were his family.

231
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:16,817
The wear and tear on the
pirate's ship was quite severe,

232
00:14:16,900 --> 00:14:19,194
so they stayed here for several months.

233
00:14:19,280 --> 00:14:23,456
In that time, I, too, was able to
forge a close relationship with them.

234
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:27,297
Thus, when the day of their
departure finally arrived...

235
00:14:27,380 --> 00:14:30,027
I have a favor to ask
of you, my dear friend.

236
00:14:30,120 --> 00:14:32,577
Could you look after
Laboon for a few years?

237
00:14:32,660 --> 00:14:37,131
When my men and I have completed our
voyage around the world, we'll be back.

238
00:14:37,400 --> 00:14:38,635
Listen to me, Laboon.

239
00:14:39,260 --> 00:14:42,037
We're taking our ship to
the end of the Grand Line.

240
00:14:42,120 --> 00:14:45,649
Until we return, I need you to
wait for us here with Crocus.

241
00:14:46,080 --> 00:14:47,640
You can't come with us.

242
00:14:47,940 --> 00:14:51,058
It's too dangerous, even
for a mighty whale like you.

243
00:14:51,340 --> 00:14:54,137
Don't worry, three years
will pass by like a flash.

244
00:14:54,220 --> 00:14:58,396
And don't forget, no matter the
distance, our friendship lasts forever.

245
00:15:03,220 --> 00:15:04,720
Well, I guess it's time.

246
00:15:08,060 --> 00:15:09,260
All right, men!

247
00:15:09,460 --> 00:15:10,580
We're setting sail!

248
00:15:10,900 --> 00:15:12,560
Hoist the anchor! Yeah!

249
00:15:17,840 --> 00:15:18,670
Laboon!

250
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:23,120
You have to wait for us here
and mine Crocus, Laboon!

251
00:15:23,420 --> 00:15:26,260
Don't worry, we'll be back! I promise!

252
00:15:38,960 --> 00:15:41,600
That was 50 long years ago.

253
00:15:41,960 --> 00:15:43,060
50 years?

254
00:15:43,280 --> 00:15:46,457
So Laboon has been waiting all this
time for his friends to return?

255
00:15:46,540 --> 00:15:47,800
No wonder he's upset.

256
00:15:48,060 --> 00:15:50,589
That kind of wait would
drive anyone crazy.

257
00:16:00,440 --> 00:16:01,940
This is some canal. Wow.

258
00:16:02,140 --> 00:16:05,377
Amazing this whale's still alive
with a hole like this in his body.

259
00:16:05,460 --> 00:16:07,197
I suppose you're responsible for this?

260
00:16:07,280 --> 00:16:10,680
Just a doctor's playful
mind. Doctor? That's right.

261
00:16:10,820 --> 00:16:11,879
Legally certified.

262
00:16:12,060 --> 00:16:15,300
I also ran a clinic on
the Cape once upon a time.

263
00:16:15,640 --> 00:16:18,052
Before that, I served as a ship's doctor.

264
00:16:18,880 --> 00:16:19,710
Really?

265
00:16:19,793 --> 00:16:21,037
That's great! Then join us!

266
00:16:21,120 --> 00:16:22,120
We need a doctor!

267
00:16:22,220 --> 00:16:23,137
Don't be ridiculous.

268
00:16:23,220 --> 00:16:25,660
I no longer have that kind of energy.

269
00:16:26,320 --> 00:16:28,777
Exploring the seas is a young man's bird!

270
00:16:28,860 --> 00:16:31,417
What kind of a doctor hollows
out the inside of a whale?

271
00:16:31,500 --> 00:16:35,297
Maybe it's part of the treatment
the girl is right with a creature of

272
00:16:35,380 --> 00:16:38,857
this size It's virtually impossible to
treat it from the outside therefore.

273
00:16:38,940 --> 00:16:40,940
I devised a more drastic solution.

274
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:42,480
Here's the exit

275
00:17:07,220 --> 00:17:08,460
What is it?

276
00:17:10,460 --> 00:17:12,857
Don't you think 50 years is a bit extreme?

277
00:17:12,940 --> 00:17:15,557
Those pirates sure know how
to test someone's patience.

278
00:17:15,640 --> 00:17:16,817
Idiot, don't you get it?

279
00:17:16,900 --> 00:17:18,300
This is the Grand Line.

280
00:17:18,740 --> 00:17:20,040
His friends are dead.

281
00:17:20,420 --> 00:17:21,817
That whale can wait forever.

282
00:17:21,900 --> 00:17:23,018
They won't be back.

283
00:17:23,540 --> 00:17:25,277
Why are you all being so pessimistic?

284
00:17:25,360 --> 00:17:26,557
You don't know that they're dead.

285
00:17:26,640 --> 00:17:28,377
They could still come back for him.

286
00:17:28,460 --> 00:17:30,017
Come on, have some sympathy, will you?

287
00:17:30,100 --> 00:17:31,924
I thought it was very touching.

288
00:17:32,160 --> 00:17:35,757
I mean, a whale still believing a promise
his friends made despite all these years?

289
00:17:35,840 --> 00:17:38,340
That's a true bond, right, Pops? Indeed.

290
00:17:38,960 --> 00:17:41,640
But the lesson here is
that reality is cruel.

291
00:17:42,280 --> 00:17:43,986
Laboon's cohorts aren't dead.

292
00:17:44,380 --> 00:17:46,677
The truth is they abandoned their quest.

293
00:17:46,760 --> 00:17:50,160
I learned that they turned
tail and left the Grand Line.

294
00:17:50,380 --> 00:17:51,498
I don't believe it.

295
00:17:51,620 --> 00:17:54,320
I mean, why abandon him like that?

296
00:17:55,480 --> 00:17:57,837
You say they ran away from the Grand Line.

297
00:17:57,920 --> 00:18:00,417
Do you know if they made it
through the Calm Belt alive?

298
00:18:00,500 --> 00:18:03,057
I can't claim to know
exactly what became of them,

299
00:18:03,140 --> 00:18:05,846
but I doubt they'll ever
come back here again.

300
00:18:06,280 --> 00:18:08,809
Seasons, weather,
currents, wind direction.

301
00:18:08,900 --> 00:18:13,076
On the Grand Line, these elements
seem to defy the very laws of nature.

302
00:18:13,520 --> 00:18:18,540
Common sense is useless, and the terror
of that fact can be quite overwhelming.

303
00:18:18,860 --> 00:18:21,037
So if I've got this straight,
what you're saying is?

304
00:18:21,120 --> 00:18:22,757
These pirates were a bunch of cowards!

305
00:18:22,840 --> 00:18:24,717
Who cared more about
saving their own asses?

306
00:18:24,800 --> 00:18:26,859
than keeping a promise to a friend.

307
00:18:28,260 --> 00:18:31,120
Why would anyone abandon
such a loyal creature?

308
00:18:31,480 --> 00:18:32,500
Just look at him.

309
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:34,640
It's cruelty on a grand scale.

310
00:18:35,040 --> 00:18:37,577
But if you've known all this,
why haven't you told him?

311
00:18:37,660 --> 00:18:39,757
Laboon can obviously communicate with you.

312
00:18:39,840 --> 00:18:42,517
He seems to understand humans
quite well for a whale.

313
00:18:42,600 --> 00:18:45,900
I did tell him, down to
the last miserable detail.

314
00:18:46,260 --> 00:18:49,417
I know this isn't what you want
to hear, but it's the truth.

315
00:18:49,500 --> 00:18:51,480
Your friends left the Grand Line.

316
00:18:51,760 --> 00:18:54,357
The bond you shared with
each other was real.

317
00:18:54,440 --> 00:18:58,520
Just as real as the promise they
made to return for you one day.

318
00:19:04,160 --> 00:19:06,520
But still, face the facts.

319
00:19:06,800 --> 00:19:08,860
Those men are never coming back.

320
00:19:29,220 --> 00:19:31,697
Laboon, please, I say
this for your own good.

321
00:19:31,780 --> 00:19:33,420
They're not coming back!

322
00:19:42,360 --> 00:19:45,080
I understand that you
don't want to accept it.

323
00:19:45,820 --> 00:19:48,960
But you need to realize
that this is how things are.

324
00:19:50,200 --> 00:19:51,030
Listen?

325
00:19:55,660 --> 00:19:56,490
Laboon!

326
00:20:07,020 --> 00:20:10,900
That was the day that Laboon
began to howl at Reverse Mountain.

327
00:20:11,420 --> 00:20:15,280
Soon after he started slamming
his body against the red line.

328
00:20:15,680 --> 00:20:19,197
It's as if he believes that wall
is what keeps his friends away?

329
00:20:19,280 --> 00:20:23,057
And by breaking it, he can
clear the path for their return!

330
00:20:23,140 --> 00:20:25,377
I admire his dedication.

331
00:20:25,460 --> 00:20:26,897
Never gave up on him.

332
00:20:26,980 --> 00:20:31,277
He refuses to believe me because then
he'd have to admit that it's over!

333
00:20:31,360 --> 00:20:33,997
And that terrifies him more than anything.

334
00:20:34,080 --> 00:20:36,777
There's no way for him to
go back to the West Blue.

335
00:20:36,860 --> 00:20:40,277
So what we have is a paradox
of tragic proportions?

336
00:20:40,360 --> 00:20:44,037
Laboon is a lost soul dying
to live with his friends.

337
00:20:44,120 --> 00:20:46,897
But won't stop killing
himself to reach them.

338
00:20:46,980 --> 00:20:47,840
Tragedy.

339
00:20:48,480 --> 00:20:50,697
Still, for all the grief those
pirates put him through,?

340
00:20:50,780 --> 00:20:54,251
they did the same to you by
saddling you with their burden.

341
00:20:55,540 --> 00:20:58,740
I mean, you've done enough
for Laboon. What about you?

342
00:20:58,920 --> 00:21:00,744
The scars on his hide are deep.

343
00:21:01,040 --> 00:21:03,099
Those in his heart are even deeper.

344
00:21:03,200 --> 00:21:06,612
He needs someone to tend his
wounds, and I'm all he's got.

345
00:21:06,800 --> 00:21:09,837
For years, he's battered himself
while I've patched him up.

346
00:21:09,920 --> 00:21:11,979
A strange friendship, but it works.

347
00:21:14,280 --> 00:21:16,477
What the hell is that idiot up to?

348
00:21:16,560 --> 00:21:18,257
We gotta watch him every second.

349
00:21:18,340 --> 00:21:20,717
We've been watching
him, look what happens.

350
00:21:20,800 --> 00:21:22,660
Gum-Gum!

351
00:21:23,800 --> 00:21:25,300
Flowing Rage Lens!

352
00:21:26,460 --> 00:21:28,757
You think he's aware that that's our mast?

353
00:21:28,840 --> 00:21:30,820
And now it's on top of a whale.

354
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:32,100
Why'd he do that?

355
00:21:32,660 --> 00:21:34,840
Hey, Luffy, stop tearing up the ship!

356
00:21:41,440 --> 00:21:42,520
You pass!

357
00:21:42,880 --> 00:21:43,720
Luffy, stop!

358
00:21:54,440 --> 00:21:56,960
The boy! He's dead! Oh, don't worry.

359
00:21:57,200 --> 00:21:59,788
It'll take way more
than that to kill Luffy.

360
00:22:04,120 --> 00:22:04,950
Gotcha!

361
00:22:11,640 --> 00:22:13,405
What does he think he's doing?

362
00:22:16,860 --> 00:22:20,389
You want to let us in on your
plan before you kill yourself?

363
00:22:24,820 --> 00:22:25,700
It's a draw.

364
00:22:30,880 --> 00:22:32,440
I'm stronger than I look.

365
00:22:33,900 --> 00:22:36,841
I can always tell when
someone's itching to fight.

366
00:22:36,960 --> 00:22:40,078
Well, if you want a battle,
I'll gladly give you one.

367
00:22:40,200 --> 00:22:42,357
Your shipmates used to
spar with you, didn't they?

368
00:22:42,440 --> 00:22:43,317
And you miss it.

369
00:22:43,400 --> 00:22:46,047
Well, I can rival anything
they threw at you.

370
00:22:46,600 --> 00:22:47,480
Tell you what,

371
00:22:47,780 --> 00:22:51,417
after me and my friends travel the Grand
Line, we'll come back here and find you.

372
00:22:51,500 --> 00:22:54,029
And then you better be
ready for a rematch.

373
00:23:08,060 --> 00:23:09,560
Hey, not bad!

374
00:23:10,660 --> 00:23:13,877
Consider this a symbol of my
promise to come do battle with you.

375
00:23:13,960 --> 00:23:15,997
Of course, it's a rush paint job,

376
00:23:16,080 --> 00:23:18,377
so you'll have to be careful
and avoid hitting your head,

377
00:23:18,460 --> 00:23:20,401
or you'll rub it off, understand?

378
00:23:24,080 --> 00:23:24,910
Goodwill!

379
00:23:27,540 --> 00:23:28,440
Where are they?

380
00:23:28,620 --> 00:23:29,480
Where are who?

381
00:23:29,760 --> 00:23:31,584
Oh, you mean those two weirdos?

382
00:23:31,720 --> 00:23:33,020
I guess they escaped.

383
00:23:33,280 --> 00:23:35,617
Must have happened when we
were distracted by Laboon.

384
00:23:35,700 --> 00:23:37,053
I wonder who they were.

385
00:23:39,780 --> 00:23:42,560
Miss Wednesday, what
are we going to do now?

386
00:23:45,020 --> 00:23:48,817
First, put some distance between us and
these morons, then we report to the boss.

387
00:23:48,900 --> 00:23:51,777
Our time for completing this
mission has long since run out.

388
00:23:51,860 --> 00:23:53,297
I can hear the others now.

389
00:23:53,380 --> 00:23:55,677
They're probably already
calling us traitors.

390
00:23:55,760 --> 00:23:57,577
I know, but without our weapons,

391
00:23:57,660 --> 00:24:00,257
we've no choice but to go back
to the office and start over.

392
00:24:00,340 --> 00:24:02,720
And this time, no one will stop us!

393
00:24:05,460 --> 00:24:07,640
Oh no, I couldn't have! What is it?

394
00:24:08,960 --> 00:24:10,780
It's... gone. What?

395
00:24:13,180 --> 00:24:14,437
What's this thing?

396
00:24:14,520 --> 00:24:17,257
How could you have lost the log? Pose?

397
00:24:17,340 --> 00:24:18,337
I don't know.

398
00:24:18,420 --> 00:24:20,057
I must have dropped it somewhere.

399
00:24:20,140 --> 00:24:22,797
But we're not going anywhere without it.

400
00:24:22,880 --> 00:24:24,217
No.

401
00:24:24,300 --> 00:24:28,060
This can't. It won't be happening!

402
00:24:34,480 --> 00:24:35,320
Oh no!

403
00:24:35,720 --> 00:24:37,040
The Unluckies!

404
00:24:37,240 --> 00:24:40,560
We're out of time! Mr.
Thirteen! It's Friday!

405
00:24:41,240 --> 00:24:42,400
Don't be hasty!

406
00:24:42,620 --> 00:24:43,837
Please, we can explain!

407
00:24:43,920 --> 00:24:46,096
We'll check in with Buzz immediately!

408
00:25:01,260 --> 00:25:02,090
Okay, then,

409
00:25:02,173 --> 00:25:03,917
I finally have some
peace and quiet so I can

410
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,471
work on a plan for the Grand Line journey.

411
00:25:10,100 --> 00:25:13,760
I don't think I forgot about
you, elephant bluefin tuna.

412
00:25:16,420 --> 00:25:17,880
Gee, that's my life.

413
00:25:18,040 --> 00:25:20,217
If he breaks it, then
I put it back together.

414
00:25:20,300 --> 00:25:22,397
Hey, get off your lazy
butt and give me a hand.

415
00:25:22,480 --> 00:25:25,186
This ship isn't going
to fix itself, you know.

416
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:31,780
You mind? Keep it down.

417
00:25:31,920 --> 00:25:33,817
Is there some problem, my dearest Nami?

418
00:25:33,900 --> 00:25:35,957
If it's food you're
worried about, say no more.

419
00:25:36,040 --> 00:25:36,870
Someone say food?

420
00:25:36,953 --> 00:25:37,783
You idiot!

421
00:25:37,920 --> 00:25:39,214
The compass is broken.

422
00:25:39,500 --> 00:25:40,800
The needle just spins.

423
00:25:40,920 --> 00:25:41,750
It won't stop.

424
00:25:41,833 --> 00:25:42,897
Oh, yeah, you're right.

425
00:25:42,980 --> 00:25:45,097
I don't know about you,
but I'm getting dizzy.

426
00:25:45,180 --> 00:25:46,380
Whoa, this is cool.

427
00:25:47,360 --> 00:25:50,077
It is sadly apparent that
none of you possess the

428
00:25:50,160 --> 00:25:52,677
slightest knowledge about
how things work here.

429
00:25:52,760 --> 00:25:54,560
Did you all come here to die?

430
00:25:55,720 --> 00:25:56,557
Great spread.

431
00:25:56,640 --> 00:25:57,557
It's for everyone.

432
00:25:57,640 --> 00:25:59,917
There is nothing wrong with your compass.

433
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:03,537
The Grand Line has little
regard for the rules of the sea.

434
00:26:03,620 --> 00:26:06,297
Common sense is useless in this place.

435
00:26:06,380 --> 00:26:07,717
The needle?

436
00:26:07,800 --> 00:26:08,997
Why does it spin?

437
00:26:09,080 --> 00:26:12,397
Because we are surrounded by
a powerful magnetic field.

438
00:26:12,480 --> 00:26:15,477
The islands here are riddled
with magnetic minerals,

439
00:26:15,560 --> 00:26:17,757
which cause all sorts of abnormalities.

440
00:26:17,840 --> 00:26:21,940
Also, the winds and currents
lack constancy in their patterns.

441
00:26:22,480 --> 00:26:25,833
As a navigator, I'm sure you
can appreciate this dilemma.

442
00:26:26,120 --> 00:26:30,277
So then, if we don't have a way to tell
direction, this whole trip is hopeless.

443
00:26:30,360 --> 00:26:33,140
I don't know what to say. We're screwed.

444
00:26:33,320 --> 00:26:35,937
Hey, you're the navigator,
so navigate us out of here.

445
00:26:36,020 --> 00:26:37,079
This is delicious.

446
00:26:37,200 --> 00:26:38,277
You ought to try some.

447
00:26:38,360 --> 00:26:41,397
But all of you just shut up for five
seconds so I can figure this out.

448
00:26:41,480 --> 00:26:44,937
This noise of the elephant tuna
is my favorite part, tasty.

449
00:26:45,020 --> 00:26:47,717
If you have any hope of
navigating the Grand Line,

450
00:26:47,800 --> 00:26:49,777
you're going to need a
Log Pose to guide you.

451
00:26:49,860 --> 00:26:50,690
Log Pose?

452
00:26:50,960 --> 00:26:52,097
I've never heard of it.

453
00:26:52,180 --> 00:26:56,077
It's a specially designed compass
that can record levels of magnetism.

454
00:26:56,160 --> 00:26:57,454
Is it strange looking?

455
00:26:57,620 --> 00:26:59,880
Why, yes, it does have an odd shape.

456
00:27:00,140 --> 00:27:01,316
Something like this?

457
00:27:01,600 --> 00:27:02,760
Yes, exactly.

458
00:27:03,120 --> 00:27:07,885
Only a fool courting disaster would attempt
to travel the Grand Line without one.

459
00:27:09,380 --> 00:27:10,210
Understood.

460
00:27:10,460 --> 00:27:11,931
Excuse me for one second.

461
00:27:12,800 --> 00:27:13,630
So.

462
00:27:14,180 --> 00:27:17,415
Why didn't you tell me you
had one of those log things?

463
00:27:17,960 --> 00:27:20,440
I found it on deck just laying there.

464
00:27:20,620 --> 00:27:23,117
Those two weirdos must have
dropped it when they escaped.

465
00:27:23,200 --> 00:27:24,030
Did they?

466
00:27:24,113 --> 00:27:27,260
Why'd you hit me? Felt like it. Oh, okay.

467
00:27:28,580 --> 00:27:31,297
Huh, but there isn't a
dial face on this thing.

468
00:27:31,380 --> 00:27:32,477
You don't need one.

469
00:27:32,560 --> 00:27:36,317
It's a special brand of magnetism that
runs through the Grand Line Islands,

470
00:27:36,400 --> 00:27:40,577
complete with its own set of rules
that makes it possible to get around.

471
00:27:40,660 --> 00:27:43,777
By recording the level of
magnetism between two islands,

472
00:27:43,860 --> 00:27:46,880
you can chart a route from
one island to the next.

473
00:27:47,160 --> 00:27:49,940
The Log Pose method is
really the only choice,

474
00:27:50,160 --> 00:27:54,631
since the magnetism here presents huge
problems for conventional navigation.

475
00:27:54,720 --> 00:27:58,057
As it turns out, Reverse Mountain
is a great starting point.

476
00:27:58,140 --> 00:28:00,880
It produces seven levels of magnetism,

477
00:28:01,200 --> 00:28:04,017
but it doesn't matter which
one you choose to start with.

478
00:28:04,100 --> 00:28:07,937
Eventually, all of them will pull
against each other and form one route.

479
00:28:08,020 --> 00:28:12,077
And the island at the end
of that route is Raftel,

480
00:28:12,160 --> 00:28:15,000
The last and most mysterious
in the Grand Line.

481
00:28:15,240 --> 00:28:18,717
Its very existence was confirmed
by the King of the Pirates,

482
00:28:18,800 --> 00:28:22,918
the only known person to have traveled
the Grand Line in its entirety.

483
00:28:23,340 --> 00:28:25,037
Raftel. That's it!

484
00:28:25,120 --> 00:28:26,877
That must be where the
One Piece is hidden!

485
00:28:26,960 --> 00:28:28,877
That's the most prominent theory.

486
00:28:28,960 --> 00:28:32,372
But no one's been able to set
foot on Raftel Island since.

487
00:28:33,660 --> 00:28:35,940
Don't worry, we'll know soon enough.

488
00:28:38,660 --> 00:28:39,957
Well, I'm satisfied.

489
00:28:40,040 --> 00:28:41,157
You guys ready to go?

490
00:28:41,240 --> 00:28:43,240
I told you that was for all of us!

491
00:28:43,400 --> 00:28:45,120
He even ate the bones.

492
00:28:45,320 --> 00:28:46,160
A Log Pose.

493
00:28:46,340 --> 00:28:48,057
We have to take special care of this.

494
00:28:48,140 --> 00:28:50,777
It's the key to finding the
greatest treasure of all time.

495
00:28:50,860 --> 00:28:53,937
You greedy, bottomless pit, you
ate the meal I made for Nami.

496
00:28:54,020 --> 00:28:56,840
Well, I hope you left
room for your dessert!

497
00:29:13,160 --> 00:29:13,990
Ow.

498
00:29:14,073 --> 00:29:15,297
Why's everyone hitting me?

499
00:29:15,380 --> 00:29:16,210
You never think of anyone
but yourself and...

500
00:29:16,293 --> 00:29:17,620
Sanchi? Yes?

501
00:29:19,280 --> 00:29:22,692
Both of you, get out of my
sight before I wring your neck!

502
00:29:27,220 --> 00:29:28,620
Okay, so,

503
00:29:28,980 --> 00:29:32,657
correct me if I'm wrong, but doesn't the
Log Pose work better when it's not broken?

504
00:29:32,740 --> 00:29:33,570
Oh, no.

505
00:29:33,653 --> 00:29:36,000
Our Log Pose is ruined. Calm yourselves.

506
